--  Configuration pragmas applied ONLY when proving, never when building.
--
--  Why this file exists
--  --------------------
--  SPARK RM 9(2) refuses to analyse any tasking construct unless the
--  partition commits to a restricted concurrency profile. A protected
--  object counts as a tasking construct even with no tasks anywhere in the
--  program, so SPARKTLS.Session_Cache -- the reference ticket store, which
--  is one protected object and nothing else -- could not be SPARK_Mode =>
--  On without these two pragmas. With them it verifies; without them
--  gnatprove reports "tasking in SPARK requires Ravenscar profile" on every
--  protected operation before looking at a single VC.
--
--  Why it is NOT in sparktls.gpr
--  -----------------------------
--  pragma Profile is partition-wide. Putting it in the project file would
--  push Ravenscar/Jorvik onto every application that builds against this
--  library -- forbidding dynamic priorities, most of Ada.Calendar, entry
--  queues longer than one caller, and much else -- for the sake of one
--  optional child package that most callers never touch. A library has no
--  business dictating its consumers' concurrency profile.
--
--  ci/prove.sh appends this file via -cargs -gnatec=, so it reaches
--  gnatprove and nothing else. `alr build` is unaffected: consumers get an
--  ordinary protected object with ordinary Ada semantics, and the profile
--  imposes no runtime cost or ordering constraint on them.
--
--  What this does and does not buy
--  -------------------------------
--  The absence-of-runtime-error proofs for the protected bodies (ranges,
--  indices, overflow) are profile-independent -- they hold regardless of
--  how the final partition is built. Data-race freedom likewise comes from
--  Ada's protected-object semantics, not from SPARK.
--
--  What DOES depend on the profile holding in the final link is SPARK's
--  scheduling-level reasoning: priority-ceiling correctness and
--  deadlock freedom. A consumer who builds without Ravenscar/Jorvik does
--  not inherit those two guarantees. That is an honest limitation and is
--  stated in the Session_Cache spec rather than glossed over.
--
--  Jorvik rather than Ravenscar: Jorvik (Ada 2022) relaxes Ravenscar's
--  hardest restrictions (relative delays, entry queue length, non-blocking
--  ceilings) while keeping the analysability SPARK needs. Session_Cache has
--  no entries and no delays, so it satisfies either; Jorvik is chosen
--  because it is the weaker claim and therefore the more honest one.

pragma Profile (Jorvik);
pragma Partition_Elaboration_Policy (Sequential);
